WEBVTT

cue-VID_U3_04-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Clearleaf Cleaning makes eco refill sachets and wants to sell them abroad. A marketing strategy is a plan with a marketing objective and details of the marketing mix that aims to achieve it. On the globe, boxes leave the home country toward three new destinations. The team must pick customers, set a position, align the four Ps, respect local rules and weigh legal controls. This video follows that journey, then compares one global message with adapted local offers. The final call must consider both sides.

cue-VID_U3_04-2
00:00:35.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Targeting starts with a segment: eco focused households who buy refills each month. Positioning places the brand as affordable and trustworthy, not the cheapest. The customer journey animates as a path: awareness, interest, purchase, then loyalty. At each step the firm places a cue, from a social post to a labelled pack. Because buyers compare refills on the shelf, therefore clear labelling reduces hesitation, so more shoppers reach purchase. Marketing works when the message follows the customer, not the factory.

cue-VID_U3_04-3
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Marketing strategy means the four Ps fit together. A refill product, a fair price, local shop distribution and honest promotion form a connected set. The tiles snap into a single frame around the chosen segment. If the price is high while the message says budget, shoppers feel confused. If promotion promises green benefits but packaging wastes plastic, trust falls. Coherence turns a plan into a position. Because each element reinforces the others, therefore the brand sounds consistent, so customers remember it.

cue-VID_U3_04-4
00:01:41.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Entering overseas markets can increase sales and spread risk. Clearleaf Cleaning earns two million dollars at home; a new country adds six hundred thousand, lifting revenue thirty percent and cutting dependence on one market to about seventy seven percent. The map lights up. But cultural differences, lack of knowledge and legal requirements raise difficulty. Customers abroad may prefer bottles, not sachets. Local rules may demand different labels. The gains are real, and so are the costs of learning a new market.

cue-VID_U3_04-5
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Cross border trade can face restrictions. A tariff is a tax on imports: a ten dollar refill gains a two dollar charge, so the landed cost becomes twelve dollars. A quota is a limit on the physical quantity of a product that can be imported, shown by a gate counter that stops at a fixed number. Rules and standards can demand extra tests. These controls protect local producers but raise prices and reduce choice. A firm must price the extra cost into its plan before it enters.

cue-VID_U3_04-6
00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Legal controls on marketing are laws passed by government to protect consumers from faulty goods, unsafe goods and misleading promotion. Three shields rise. Misleading promotion: a claim of completely safe needs evidence, or it is struck through. Pricing: the shown price must include compulsory charges, so there is no surprise at the till. Labelling: ingredients, origin and instructions must be clear. Consumer protection also covers complaints, safety and returns. Clearleaf rewrites its pack claims before shipping, and the shield stays intact.

cue-VID_U3_04-7
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Regulation has two edges. Compliance costs Clearleaf Cleaning thirty thousand dollars and six weeks of checks, which slows a campaign. Yet clear rules build trust, cut returns from five percent to two percent, and protect reputable firms from unfair rivals. The scales balance a cost coin against a trust shield. Because rules stop rivals making false claims, therefore honest brands look stronger, so customers buy with confidence. A short term expense becomes long term protection.

cue-VID_U3_04-8
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Last decision: standardised or adapted marketing? One global product and message keeps costs low and the brand consistent. Adapting language, pack size and price fits local habits, but every change adds cost and complexity. On the split screen, the left column counts savings; the right column counts relevance. Because refills depend on local words and rules, therefore Clearleaf adapts labels and price, so it standardises the core product only. A smart strategy blends both sides instead of choosing blindly.

